WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:14.346
This is the Week 9 fingerboard deck: 96 × 28 mm, sized for the kit trucks. We'll build it the Lesson 2.1 way, just smaller, and in millimeters.

00:00:14.896 --> 00:00:20.341
Open the document menu, choose Workspace units, and set length to millimeters.

00:00:20.891 --> 00:00:28.798
This part is new. Make a variable called #bolt: the bolt diameter you measured. Ours is 2 mm.

00:00:29.348 --> 00:00:35.315
Make a variable called #clear: the clearance from your Week 5 cube. Ours is 0.3 mm.

00:00:35.865 --> 00:00:41.473
And #hole = #bolt + #clear. That's 2.3 mm.

00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:48.919
Now, just like Lesson 2.1. Start a sketch on the Top plane, and press N to look straight down.

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:52.941
Press R and draw a center-point rectangle on the origin.

00:00:53.491 --> 00:00:58.820
Press C for a circle. Click the midpoint of the left side. Wait for the yellow box.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.209
Drag to the corner and click. Do the same on the right. The circles make the round nose and tail.

00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:12.203
Press D. Dimension the right edge of the rectangle: 28 mm wide, to fit the kit trucks.

00:01:12.753 --> 00:01:18.407
Then click the left circle and the right circle. Move below: the dimension goes tip to tip.

00:01:18.957 --> 00:01:21.360
Click to place it and type 96.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:25.231
Every line is black. The sketch is fully defined.

00:01:25.781 --> 00:01:35.870
Extrude it. Click Sketch 1 in the list to pick every region. Lesson 2.1 used 10 mm. A fingerboard deck is 2.5 mm.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.102
There's the blank deck. Next, the bolt holes.

00:01:39.652 --> 00:01:43.750
Start a new sketch on the top face of the deck, and press N.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:52.369
Press R. Move right of the origin until the yellow dotted line appears, and click. That keeps the holes centered.

00:01:52.919 --> 00:01:57.969
Click again to place the corner. Each corner of this rectangle becomes a bolt hole.

00:01:58.519 --> 00:02:07.587
Dimension it with your truck numbers. Ours: 25 from the origin, 8 along, 6 across. Use your own measurements.

00:02:08.137 --> 00:02:10.134
Search for Mirror in the toolbar.

00:02:10.684 --> 00:02:19.588
Pick the Right plane as the mirror line, then the four rectangle lines. The second truck has its rectangle too. Finish the sketch.

00:02:20.138 --> 00:02:23.946
Open the Hole tool. Choose Simple. Termination: Through all.

00:02:24.496 --> 00:02:27.573
For the diameter, type #hole, not a number.

00:02:28.123 --> 00:02:37.957
Click the four corners of each rectangle. Eight holes. Re-measure your bolt? Change one variable and all eight update.

00:02:38.507 --> 00:02:42.768
Here are the holes: two sets of four, one for each truck.

00:02:43.318 --> 00:02:52.257
Last, the fillet. Press Shift+F, pick the top and bottom edges. Lesson 2.1 used 0.125 in. We use 0.5 mm.

00:02:52.807 --> 00:03:02.037
Here's the deck: 96 × 28 × 2.5 mm, 8 holes. It sits flat, so it prints with no supports.

00:03:02.587 --> 00:03:13.176
To print it, right-click the part and choose Export. Pick 3MF and export. Using STL? Set units to millimeters first.
